  CaseProcessing.Tests rose from 380 to 539: 159 new end-to-end tests.
Acceptance criteria: T04 mechanical acceptance met, all through the public API.
  - AC-2: the full chain runs envelope -> ExtractionEnvelopeValidator.Validate ->
    T4CaseProcessor.Process, and the result's line entries equal T4LineMapper's
    own output for the parsed slip. The payload reaches the parser
    reference-identical, so no step between the two is hand-written.
  - AC-38: a provider holding a known in-memory map whose destinations are
    deliberately not CRA lines (TEST-LINE-A/B/C) produces exactly those line
    numbers, and the same extraction through two different maps yields disjoint
    destination sets - the output follows the config, not the code. A source scan
    additionally finds no CRA-shaped literal and no TaxLogicEngine.Mappings.*
    resource name.
  - AC-39: the module constructs no T4Slip, never names T4RawBox, parses no
    value, declares no box-code allow-list, and every `.Value` read is pinned to
    a ParseResult receiver allow-list, so a box read through `var` would fail.
    Behavioural counterpart: for a payload the parser rejects, no slip is
    produced at all.
  - AC-29 through AC-33: the full mandatory PII matrix - 5 plants x 11 slots,
    each planted separately, each driven through the accepted pipeline and all 12
    rejection families. Box 12 = 123456789 on an accepted pipeline exposes only
    ***-**-789. Includes AC-31 (no SIN property, SIN type, masking helper, or
    RecipientIdentifier construction; exactly one RecipientIdentifier reachable
    from a result) and AC-32 (no member exposes T4RawInput/T4RawBox, no such
    object is reachable from the result object graph, and the caller still holds
    the payload).
  - AC-7: replay equality over the accepted pipeline and every rejection family,
    five replays each, asserting equal error ordering rather than set equality;
    two distinct-but-equivalent extractions agree; five replays mutate neither
    the extraction nor its payload.
Design decision worth review:
  The PII surfaces are split in two, deliberately. On the omission surfaces (the
  result's ToString, the JSON, the mapping and its line entries, every
  ValidationError) not even a masked remnant may appear. The engine's masked
  RecipientIdentifier on T4Slip.Sin is the one permitted SIN representation and
  is asserted to show only the last three digits. A single combined rule would
  either forbid the masking the contract requires or permit a remnant where
  nothing belongs.
Two gaps found by mutation testing and closed (commit 2fcc35f):
  1. The AC-32 surface check compared member types against a fixed list of
     spellings, so a member typed T4RawBox[] would have passed. Replaced with a
     recursive detector that looks through array element types and generic
     arguments at any depth, plus a test of the detector itself against six
     positive and four negative spellings.
  2. AC-7's ordering claim was vacuous: every rejection family emitted exactly
     one error, and a one-element sequence is in order whatever the module does
     to it. Randomly reordering the accumulated stage 3/4 errors between runs
     passed the entire suite. Added two multi-error pipelines
     (MultipleParserDefects: all three required boxes absent plus a malformed
     amount; YearAndStructureDefects: an unsupported year together with a
     structural defect so stages 3 and 4 accumulate) and a sweep guard asserting
     at least two families emit more than one error.
Mutation evidence (the arms are not vacuous):
  - Leak the masked SIN into the result's ToString() -> 113 tests fail.
  - Drop [JsonIgnore] from SourceReference -> 62 tests fail.
  - Expose the raw boxes off the result as an array -> 61 tests fail.
  - Leak DocumentId into the result's ToString() -> 12 tests fail.
  - Randomly reorder accumulated stage 3/4 errors -> 3 tests fail (0 before the
    fix above; this is the mutation that exposed gap 2).
  - Reverse the parser's error order -> 1 test fails.
  - Trim attribution on the way out -> 1 test fails.
Sweep guards (so a shrinking matrix cannot silently reduce coverage):
  PlantMatrix_CoversEverySlotAndEveryPlant pins 11 slots, 5 plants, 55
  combinations, 13 pipelines, and asserts every declared pipeline actually runs
  and every rejection family actually rejects.
  ReplaySweep_ActuallyCoversAnAcceptedRunAndEveryRejectionFamily pins 1 success
  and 12 failures, and that at least two families emit multiple errors.
  RawPayloadTypeDetector_CatchesEveryWayOfSpellingIt guards the AC-32 detector.
  SourceScanner_SeesEveryProductionFileAndStillMatchesAKnownPattern guards the
  source walk used by AC-38 and AC-39.
